    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d702">

                <pb facs="0790" n="697" sameAs="frus:frus1935v04/pg697"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">838.51/3058: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Haiti</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Chapin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Port-au-Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-29T14:00:00-05:00">November 29, 1935—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:44 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>111. In a further interview with the Minister for Foreign Affairs this morning he
                    stated that as a result of our conversation yesterday <pb facs="0791" n="698"
                        xml:id="pg698"/> he had been authorized by the President to show me the
                    letter from Debachy to President Vincent of which an extract was forwarded in
                    the Legation’s despatch No. 42 of October 21.<anchor n="83" xml:id="d702-f1"
                    /></p>
                <p>The letter which was brief contained nothing of great interest except that it set
                    amount due of retiring American loan at 165,000,000 francs. At present rate of
                    exchange this is practically sufficient providing Haitian Treasury investments
                    amounting to $640,000 are deducted from amount outstanding of the 1922 loan.</p>
                <p>Although he stated that he knew of no other correspondence in existence, I desire
                    to point out that the President who handled the whole affair personally has not
                    given this assurance.</p>
                <p>The Foreign Minister informed me Haitian Government did not intend to make a
                    reply to Mayard’s last cable pending news of some reaction from the Department
                    of State and has assumed that Debachy must carry out contract as stipulated and
                    that any delay will be for his account and penalty clause thus become operative
                    against him if first payment not made December 3.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chapin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
